           "source_section": "Definition",
           "quote": "Assume AC for the supplied existence theorem. Write $E[X\\mid\\mathcal G]$ for the unique class in $L^1(\\Omega,\\mathcal G,P|_{\\mathcal G})$ consisting of conditional-expectation versions of $X$. A chosen real $\\mathcal G$-measurable representative is a **version**. Equalities and inequalities involving these classes mean almost-sure equalities and inequalities.\n\nExistence is [[thm-conditional-expectation-exists-by-radon-nikodym]] and uniqueness is [[lem-conditional-expectation-is-unique-almost-surely]], applied to [[def-conditional-expectation-given-a-sigma-algebra]]. The quotient is [[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]]. If $X=X^{\\ast}$ almost surely, [[thm-the-lebesgue-integral-respects-almost-everywhere-equality]] gives identical event integrals, so the output class is independent of the input representative. The [[def-axiom-of-choice]] assumption is inherited from RN existence.",
           "uses": [
+            "1.1",
             "2.1"
           ]
         },
@@ -1057,6 +1058,7 @@
           "source_section": "Statement",
           "quote": "Assume AC for existence. If $\\mathcal H\\subseteq\\mathcal G\\subseteq\\mathcal F$ and $X\\in L^1(P)$, then $E[E[X\\mid\\mathcal G]\\mid\\mathcal H]=E[X\\mid\\mathcal H]$ and $E[E[X\\mid\\mathcal H]\\mid\\mathcal G]=E[X\\mid\\mathcal H]$ almost surely. Also, if a version of $E[X\\mid\\mathcal G]$ is $\\mathcal H$-measurable, it is a version of $E[X\\mid\\mathcal H]$.",
           "uses": [
+            "1.1",
             "2.1"
           ]
         },
@@ -1066,6 +1068,7 @@
           "source_section": "Statement",
           "quote": "Let $X,Y$ be integrable real or complex random variables on one probability\nspace.\n\n1. For scalars $a,b$,\n   $$\\mathbb E[aX+bY]=a\\,\\mathbb E[X]+b\\,\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n2. If $X$ and $Y$ are real-valued and $X\\le Y$ almost surely, then\n   $$\\mathbb E[X]\\le\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n3. $$|\\mathbb E[X]|\\le\\mathbb E[|X|].$$",
           "uses": [
+            "1.1",
             "2.1"
           ]
         },
